-- Q9 [Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments · medium · Skill 2 · ans B] (g27)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments',
    $qt27$During cytokinesis in a dividing animal cell, a contractile ring tightens just beneath the plasma membrane and pinches the cell inward to form the cleavage furrow. A researcher applies a drug that prevents this ring from generating tension, and the furrow fails to deepen. The drug most likely disrupts the function of which cytoskeletal component?$qt27$,
    $op27$[{"label":"A","text":"Microtubules, which form the spindle that pulls the replicated chromosomes toward opposite poles of the cell"},{"label":"B","text":"Microfilaments, which are built from actin and serve as the track along which the motor protein myosin generates contractile force"},{"label":"C","text":"Intermediate filaments, which bear tension to maintain cell shape and anchor the nucleus and other organelles in place"},{"label":"D","text":"Tubulin dimers, which polymerize into hollow walls and resist the compressive forces acting on the cell"}]$op27$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex27$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the contractile ring that pinches the cleavage furrow during cytokinesis is built from actin microfilaments, which provide the track for myosin-driven contraction. The textbook states that microfilaments "are comprised of two globular protein intertwined strands, which we call actin. For this reason, we also call microfilaments actin filaments" and that "ATP powers actin to assemble its filamentous form, which serves as a track for the movement of a motor protein we call myosin. This enables actin to engage in cellular events requiring motion, such as cell division in eukaryotic cells" (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 5854-5873). Because the actin filaments and their associated myosin generate the tension that constricts the furrow, a drug that abolishes that contractile force points to a defect in the microfilament system. (Choice A) Microtubules do act during division, but their role is to "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell" (line 5909) as the spindle, not to constrict the surface furrow, so they are the wrong machine for the contractile ring. (Choice B) is correct: microfilaments are actin filaments that, together with myosin, generate the contractile force that deepens the cleavage furrow. (Choice C) Intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement. Their function is purely structural. They bear tension, thus maintaining the cell's shape, and anchor the nucleus and other organelles in place" (lines 5897-5898), so they cannot drive an active contraction. (Choice D) Tubulin dimers are the building blocks of microtubules, whose walls "consist of 13 polymerized dimers of α-tubulin and β-tubulin" and which "help the cell resist compression" (lines 5906-5908); they belong to the microtubule system, not the actin contractile ring. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must connect the observed loss of contractile force at the furrow to the specific cytoskeletal element (actin microfilaments plus myosin) responsible for generating that force.$ex27$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc27$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc27$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
