UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a27C$Correctly states the structural, tension-bearing role of intermediate filaments but ignores that they have no role in active movement/contraction$a27C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a27D$Substitutes the microtubule building block (tubulin) for the actin-based contractile machinery, confusing which subunit underlies the ring$a27D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments · hard · Skill 2 · ans C] (g28)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments',
    $qt28$A cultured animal cell is treated with cytochalasin, a drug that caps actin filament plus-ends and blocks further actin polymerization, just as the cell enters M phase. Spindle microtubules and their motor proteins are left fully functional. After the cell completes mitosis, which outcome is the drug MOST likely to produce?$qt28$,
    $op28$[{"label":"A","text":"The cell arrests in metaphase because its replicated chromosomes cannot be pulled toward opposite poles."},{"label":"B","text":"The cell divides into two daughter cells, but each daughter receives only a single, unpaired set of chromosomes."},{"label":"C","text":"The cell becomes a single binucleate cell because the chromosomes separate into two nuclei but the cytoplasm fails to pinch apart."},{"label":"D","text":"The cell forms two daughter cells of normal chromosome content but with no intermediate filaments to anchor their nuclei."}]$op28$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex28$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because actin microfilaments form the contractile ring that physically pinches the cytoplasm in two during cytokinesis, whereas chromosome separation is driven by spindle microtubules; blocking only actin therefore lets the nuclei divide while the cytoplasm stays whole, yielding one cell with two nuclei. The text states that "ATP powers actin to assemble its filamentous form... This enables actin to engage in cellular events requiring motion, such as cell division in eukaryotic cells" (line 5871-5872), so disabling actin polymerization halts the cytoplasmic division step. Chromosome movement, however, is a microtubule job: microtubules "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell" (line 5909), and the spindle is left intact, so karyokinesis still completes and two separate nuclei form. With sister chromatids correctly partitioned into two nuclei but the cytoplasm undivided, the result is a single binucleate cell. (Choice A) Metaphase arrest would require disrupting the spindle microtubules that align and pull chromosomes, but those are explicitly left functional, so chromosome separation proceeds. (Choice B) Producing daughters with single unpaired chromosome sets describes a missegregation/spindle failure, not a cytokinesis block; actin loss does not alter how chromosomes are partitioned. (Choice C) Correct: microtubules separate the chromosomes into two nuclei, but without the actin contractile ring the cytoplasm cannot constrict, so the two nuclei remain in one cell. (Choice D) Intermediate filaments, not microfilaments, anchor the nucleus, and they "have no role in cell movement" (line ~5896); cytochalasin targets actin, so this misattributes both the affected filament and the defect. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct roles of microfilaments and microtubules to predict the specific cellular outcome of a targeted polymerization inhibitor.$ex28$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc28$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc28$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a28A$Attributes the actin-dependent cytokinesis step to the microtubule-dependent chromosome-separation step, predicting a metaphase arrest$a28A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a28B$Confuses a cytokinesis block with a chromosome missegregation event, inventing an aneuploid daughter outcome$a28B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a28D$Swaps microfilaments for intermediate filaments by citing the true but irrelevant nucleus-anchoring role of intermediate filaments$a28D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g29)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microfilaments',
    $qt29$A reagent selectively inactivates the myosin motor in cultured intestinal epithelial cells while leaving the actin filaments themselves fully polymerized and intact. The cells normally use a contractile actin-myosin meshwork beneath the plasma membrane (the cell cortex) and also bundle actin to support their apical microvilli. After treatment, which combination of outcomes is MOST likely?$qt29$,
    $op29$[{"label":"A","text":"Both cortical contraction and microvillus support collapse, because actin filaments cannot perform either function once the myosin motor is inactivated."},{"label":"B","text":"Cortical contraction is preserved but the microvilli collapse, because the apical actin bundles depend on myosin for their structural rigidity."},{"label":"C","text":"Both cortical contraction and microvillus support are preserved, because the intact actin filaments generate contractile force on their own without a motor protein."},{"label":"D","text":"Cortical contraction is lost but the microvilli remain supported, because force generation requires actin sliding against myosin while the microvillus core depends on the actin bundle itself."}]$op29$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex29$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because microfilament-based force generation requires actin filaments to slide against the myosin motor, so inactivating myosin abolishes active cortical contraction, whereas the microvillus is held up by the actin bundle itself and so remains supported even without a working motor. The text states that "ATP powers actin to assemble its filamentous form, which serves as a track for the movement of a motor protein we call myosin," and that "When your actin and myosin filaments slide past each other, your muscles contract" (lines 5871-5874) — contraction is the product of the actin-myosin interaction, not of actin alone. The same passage notes that "Microfilaments also provide some rigidity and shape to the cell" (line 5876), a structural role the polymerized actin bundle fills without needing the motor; in the intestine these actin-cored projections are the microvilli that "increase the surface area available for absorption" (line 5384). Therefore removing only the motor splits the two functions: the motor-driven cortical contraction fails, but the static actin scaffold of the microvillus persists. (Choice A) is wrong because it treats both functions as motor-dependent; the actin core still provides "rigidity and shape" (line 5876) without myosin, so the microvilli do not collapse. (Choice B) reverses the dependency — it is the contraction, not the static microvillus bundle, that needs myosin, so preserving contraction while losing microvilli is backwards. (Choice C) is the tempting partial truth: actin is intact, but the text is explicit that contraction arises only when "actin and myosin filaments slide past each other" (line 5874), so actin cannot generate contractile force on its own without the motor. (Choice D) correctly separates motor-dependent force generation from the motor-independent structural scaffold. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that microfilament force generation depends on the actin-myosin interaction to predict which of two distinct actin-based functions survives selective loss of the motor.$ex29$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc29$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc29$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a29A$Treats every actin-based function as strictly myosin-dependent, conflating the static structural role of actin with motor-driven contraction$a29A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a29B$Reverses which function needs the motor — assigns myosin-dependence to the static microvillus bundle instead of to the contractile cortex$a29B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a29C$510-tempting trap: correctly notes actin is intact but wrongly concludes actin alone generates contractile force without a motor$a29C$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules · easy · Skill 1 · ans A] (g30)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microtubules',
    $qt30$A cell biologist images a cytoskeletal fiber that runs through the cell's interior, measures its diameter at about 25 nm, and finds that its wall is built from polymerized dimers of two globular proteins arranged around a hollow core. Based on these structural features, which fiber is being imaged, and what mechanical role does its architecture most directly serve?$qt30$,
    $op30$[{"label":"A","text":"A microtubule, a hollow tube whose walls are polymerized \\(\\alpha\\)-tubulin/\\(\\beta\\)-tubulin dimers, which helps the cell resist compression"},{"label":"B","text":"A microfilament, a hollow tube whose walls are polymerized \\(\\alpha\\)-tubulin/\\(\\beta\\)-tubulin dimers, which helps the cell resist compression"},{"label":"C","text":"A microtubule, a solid twin-stranded fiber of polymerized actin, which helps the cell resist compression"},{"label":"D","text":"A microtubule, a hollow tube whose walls are polymerized \\(\\alpha\\)-tubulin/\\(\\beta\\)-tubulin dimers, which helps the cell resist tension"}]$op30$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex30$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because a microtubule is a hollow tube whose walls are built from polymerized α-tubulin/β-tubulin dimers, and this rigid tubular architecture is what lets it resist compression. The textbook states that "microtubules are small hollow tubes. Polymerized dimers of α-tubulin and β-tubulin, two globular proteins, comprise the microtubule's walls," noting a diameter of "about 25 nm," which matches every structural feature observed. The same passage assigns the function directly: microtubules "help the cell resist compression," provide tracks for vesicles, and pull chromosomes apart during division. The hollow, wide tube is the only one of the three fiber types built from tubulin, so the structural fingerprint uniquely identifies it. (Choice A) Correct: it correctly pairs the hollow tubulin-dimer tube with resistance to compression, both stated in the text. (Choice B) Wrong identity: microfilaments are the narrowest fibers (~7 nm) made of two intertwined actin strands, not 25-nm hollow tubulin tubes, so the structure described cannot be a microfilament even though the rest of the description fits a microtubule. (Choice C) Wrong structure: it keeps the microtubule label but describes the actin makeup of a microfilament; microtubules are hollow tubulin tubes, not solid twin-stranded actin fibers. (Choice D) Wrong mechanical role: the text says microtubules resist compression, whereas resisting tension is the job of microfilaments and intermediate filaments, so swapping in "tension" misassigns the property. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the molecular composition of a microtubule and the mechanical property its hollow tubulin-tube architecture confers.$ex30$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc30$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc30$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a30B$Swaps in the name of a different cytoskeletal fiber (microfilament) while keeping the microtubule's tubulin structure$a30B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a30C$Keeps the correct fiber name but substitutes actin (microfilament composition) for tubulin$a30C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a30D$Correct structure but assigns the opposite mechanical role (tension instead of compression)$a30D$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules · medium · Skill 2 · ans B] (g31)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microtubules',
    $qt31$In a cultured neuron, microtubules radiate outward from the centrosome near the nucleus, so their minus ends are anchored centrally and their plus ends point toward the axon terminal at the cell periphery. A vesicle is loaded onto one of these microtubules by a kinesin motor. Toward which destination does the kinesin most likely carry the vesicle?$qt31$,
    $op31$[{"label":"A","text":"Toward the centrosome, because motor proteins move cargo back to the microtubule-organizing center for recycling"},{"label":"B","text":"Toward the plus end at the cell periphery, advancing the vesicle out toward the axon terminal"},{"label":"C","text":"Toward the minus end near the nucleus, tracking back along the microtubule to the cell's center"},{"label":"D","text":"In no fixed direction, because the vesicle diffuses freely once it is attached to the microtubule"}]$op31$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex31$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because a kinesin motor walks directionally toward the plus end of a microtubule, which in this neuron points away from the centrally anchored minus ends and out toward the axon terminal at the cell periphery. Microtubules are polarized tubulin polymers that "provide a track along which vesicles move through the cell," and in animal cells they originate from the centrosome, the microtubule-organizing center located near the nucleus (OpenStax Biology 2e). Because the centrosome anchors the minus ends, the plus ends extend toward the periphery; a plus-end-directed motor such as kinesin therefore drives anterograde transport away from the cell center toward the terminal. The opposite, minus-end-directed return trip is the job of a different motor (dynein), not kinesin, so direction follows directly from which end of the polarized track the motor is built to walk toward. (Choice A) Returning cargo to the centrosome/MTOC is minus-end-directed transport carried out by dynein, not by the plus-end-directed kinesin described here. (Choice B) Correct: kinesin is plus-end-directed, and the plus ends here point to the periphery, so the vesicle advances toward the axon terminal. (Choice C) Moving toward the minus end near the nucleus reverses kinesin's actual directionality and again describes dynein-type retrograde transport. (Choice D) Motor-driven transport is directed and ATP-powered along a track, not free diffusion; attachment to a kinesin imposes a specific direction rather than random movement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the polarity of the microtubule track and the known directionality of the kinesin motor to predict which way a specific vesicle will be carried.$ex31$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc31$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc31$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a31A$Attributes minus-end-directed (dynein, toward-MTOC) transport to the kinesin motor$a31A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a31C$Confuses kinesin's plus-end directionality with minus-end/centripetal movement$a31C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a31D$Treats motor-driven directed transport as passive free diffusion along the track$a31D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g32)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microtubules',
    $qt32$During mitosis, a cultured cell is treated with a drug that prevents spindle microtubules from attaching to the kinetochores of the replicated chromosomes; the drug leaves the centrosomes, the nuclear breakdown, and microtubule polymerization otherwise intact. Which outcome most directly follows for the affected chromosomes?$qt32$,
    $op32$[{"label":"A","text":"Histones detach from the DNA, so the chromatin can no longer condense into visible chromosomes."},{"label":"B","text":"The cell builds extra microfilaments to substitute for the spindle and pull the chromatids apart on schedule."},{"label":"C","text":"Sister chromatids fail to be drawn to opposite poles, so the two daughter cells do not receive equal, complete chromosome sets."},{"label":"D","text":"The chromosomes are pulled to opposite poles faster because the centrosomes compensate by generating extra pulling force."}]$op32$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex32$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the spindle microtubules are the structures that physically attach to chromosomes and pull the separated sister chromatids toward opposite poles; if they cannot attach at the kinetochores, that segregating force is lost and the chromatids are not partitioned equally between the two forming daughter cells. The text states that microtubules "pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell," identifying them as the cytoskeletal element responsible for chromosome segregation (lines 5908–5909). Disabling only the kinetochore attachment—while leaving centrosomes, nuclear breakdown, and tubulin polymerization functional—isolates the spindle's pulling role, so the predictable result is missegregation, not a structural change to chromatin or a switch to a different filament system. (Choice A) Histone–DNA association and chromatin condensation are independent of spindle attachment; the drug targets microtubule binding, not the histone-packaged chromosome, so the chromosomes remain condensed and visible. (Choice B) Microfilaments are actin-based and function in cytoplasmic streaming and cytokinesis, not in poleward chromosome movement; they do not substitute for spindle microtubules in pulling chromatids. (Choice C) Correct: with no microtubule–kinetochore attachment, the replicated sister chromatids cannot be drawn to opposite poles, so daughter cells receive unequal, incomplete chromosome sets. (Choice D) Centrosomes are microtubule-organizing centers, not an independent force generator; they cannot accelerate segregation when the very microtubules that connect to the chromosomes are detached. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must predict the downstream consequence of selectively disrupting the spindle's attachment to chromosomes rather than simply recalling what microtubules are.$ex32$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc32$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc32$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a32A$Invokes a real but unrelated chromosome fact (histone packaging) that the drug does not affect$a32A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a32B$Confuses microfilaments (actin) with microtubules as the chromosome-pulling element$a32B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a32D$Reverses the consequence—predicts faster/enhanced segregation when the structure is in fact disabled, treating the centrosome as a force generator$a32D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Cytoskeleton: Microtubules · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g33)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Cytoskeleton: Microtubules',
    $qt33$A neuron is treated with a drug that depolymerizes microtubules into free tubulin dimers while leaving actin microfilaments, intermediate filaments, and all motor proteins intact. The cell's machinery for the long-range, ATP-driven transport of membrane-bound vesicles from the cell body down the axon then stalls. Which transported cargo is MOST likely to be stranded as a direct consequence of this drug, while a comparable actin-dependent process at the cell cortex continues unaffected?$qt33$,
    $op33$[{"label":"A","text":"Cortical vesicles being shuttled the final short distance to the plasma membrane by myosin"},{"label":"B","text":"Keratin and other intermediate filaments being repositioned to anchor the nucleus"},{"label":"C","text":"Replicated chromosomes failing to separate during cytoplasmic streaming"},{"label":"D","text":"Membrane-bound organelles that depend on the microtubule track for transport down the axon"}]$op33$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex33$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because microtubules are the cytoskeletal tracks along which membrane-bound organelles and vesicles are driven through the cell; depolymerizing them removes that track and strands the cargo. The text states that microtubules "provide a track along which vesicles move through the cell, and pull replicated chromosomes to opposite ends of a dividing cell" (line 5908-5909). Because the drug eliminates only microtubules, the long-range vesicle/organelle transport that rides on those tracks halts, whereas the short-range cortical step that rides on actin filaments is independent of microtubules and keeps running. The text separately establishes that actin "serves as a track for the movement of a motor protein we call myosin" (line 5871-5872) and that intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement. Their function is purely structural" (line 5897). (Choice A) Wrong: this is the actin-dependent cortical step the stem says continues; myosin runs on actin microfilaments, which the drug leaves intact, so this cargo is not stranded. (Choice B) Wrong: repositioning intermediate filaments is a structural, non-motile process that does not ride on microtubules, and the text says intermediate filaments "have no role in cell movement," so it is not the stranded transport cargo. (Choice C) Wrong: chromosome separation does depend on microtubules, but the text ties it to "opposite ends of a dividing cell," not to cytoplasmic streaming, which is an actin-driven process — the choice fuses a microtubule role to the wrong (actin) mechanism. (Choice D) Correct: vesicle and organelle transport runs directly along the microtubule track, so removing microtubules strands this cargo while the actin-based cortical step continues. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must predict the differential cellular consequence of a microtubule-specific perturbation by mapping each candidate process to the cytoskeletal element it actually depends on.$ex33$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc33$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc33$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a33A$Attributes the microtubule-dependent transport failure to the actin/myosin cortical step that the stem explicitly says continues$a33A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a33B$Names a real intermediate-filament structural role but one with no role in motility/transport$a33B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a33C$Correctly notes microtubules move chromosomes but fuses it to actin-driven cytoplasmic streaming, the wrong mechanism$a33C$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Tissues: Connective Tissue · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g47)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Connective Tissue',
    $qt47$A pathologist views a micrograph of a tendon and observes relatively few cells separated by wide expanses of densely packed collagen fibers embedded in ground substance. Which observed feature most directly identifies this specimen as connective tissue rather than epithelial tissue?$qt47$,
    $op47$[{"label":"A","text":"The cells are joined edge-to-edge by tight junctions that seal off any intervening space."},{"label":"B","text":"The cells are sparsely dispersed within an abundant extracellular matrix that occupies most of the tissue's volume."},{"label":"C","text":"The cells rest on a basement membrane that separates them from the underlying tissue."},{"label":"D","text":"The cells are tightly packed together with little or no extracellular space between them."}]$op47$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex47$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the defining histological hallmark of connective tissue is that its cells are dispersed within an abundant extracellular matrix, whereas epithelial cells are packed together with little or no extracellular space. The text states, "Unlike epithelial tissue, which is composed of cells closely packed with little or no extracellular space in between, connective tissue cells are dispersed in a matrix. The matrix usually includes a large amount of extracellular material produced by the connective tissue cells that are embedded within it." In a tendon (dense regular connective tissue), the few resident fibroblasts are scattered among bundles of collagen fibers and ground substance that make up the bulk of the tissue, so the micrograph showing sparse cells surrounded by a large matrix is the feature that marks it as connective. (Choice A) Tight junctions that seal adjacent cells together are a feature of epithelial tissue, not connective tissue; the text notes that when epithelial cells "form a tight junction, there is no extracellular space between them," which is the opposite of the matrix-rich connective arrangement. (Choice B) is correct: connective tissue is defined by cells that "are dispersed in a matrix" containing "a large amount of extracellular material," matching the sparse-cells-in-abundant-matrix appearance described. (Choice C) A basement (basal) membrane on which cells rest is a structural feature associated with epithelia, not the defining property of connective tissue, and a single anchoring layer is not what distinguishes a matrix-embedded tissue. (Choice D) Cells "closely packed with little or no extracellular space between them" describes epithelial tissue ("Cells of epithelia are closely connected and are not separated by extracellular material"), which is precisely the contrast the question asks you to reject. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining histological feature that distinguishes connective tissue (cells dispersed in an abundant extracellular matrix) from epithelial tissue.$ex47$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc47$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc47$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a47A$Attributes a real epithelial junction (tight junctions) to connective tissue$a47A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a47C$Cites the basement membrane, a real epithelial feature, as the connective-tissue identifier$a47C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a47D$Gives the epithelial criterion (tightly packed cells, no extracellular space) as if it defined connective tissue$a47D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Tissues: Connective Tissue · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g48)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Connective Tissue',
    $qt48$A surgeon repairing a ruptured Achilles tendon notes that the healthy tendon must transmit the large pulling force generated by the calf muscles to the heel bone without stretching or tearing. The extracellular matrix component most directly responsible for letting the tendon withstand this tensile load is the:$qt48$,
    $op48$[{"label":"A","text":"elastic fiber, which contains elastin and snaps the tendon back to its original length after the force is removed"},{"label":"B","text":"reticular fiber, which forms a fine branching network that anchors the surrounding cells in place"},{"label":"C","text":"collagen fiber, which is built from fibrous protein subunits that give the tissue great tensile strength and resist stretching"},{"label":"D","text":"ground substance, whose hydrated proteoglycans trap water and let the matrix resist compressive loads"}]$op48$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex48$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the mechanical demand described—transmitting a large pulling (tensile) force without stretching or tearing—is precisely the demand met by collagen fibers, the dominant fiber of the dense regular connective tissue that makes up tendons. The text states that collagen fibers, "while flexible, have great tensile strength, resist stretching, and give ligaments and tendons their characteristic resilience and strength" (line 6839-6841), and that in tendons these collagen fibers run "parallel to each other, enhancing tensile strength and resistance to stretching in the direction of the fiber orientations" (line 6904-6905). The task is to map the physical load (tension) onto the matrix element whose material property resists it, so collagen—not a recoiling, branching, or hydrated component—is the correct match. (Choice A) Elastic fiber is wrong because elastin's defining property is recoil—"after being stretched or compressed, it will return to its original shape" (line 6843-6844)—which addresses returning to original length, not resisting the initial stretch under a heavy pulling load. (Choice B) Reticular fiber is wrong because, although it is built from the same protein subunits as collagen, these fibers "remain narrow and are arrayed in a branching network" that "anchor and provide structural support to the parenchyma" of soft organs like liver and spleen (line 6847-6850), a supportive scaffolding role rather than tensile load-bearing. (Choice C) Collagen fiber is correct: its straight fibrous-protein subunits confer "great tensile strength, resist stretching," exactly the property a tendon needs to transmit a pulling force (line 6839-6841). (Choice D) Ground substance is wrong because it is the matrix, not a fiber; its hydrated proteoglycans "attract and trap available moisture" (line 6852-6855) and resist compression, not the tensile pulling load described. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must connect a described mechanical demand (a tensile pulling load) to the specific matrix fiber whose material property resists that load, rather than simply recalling a fiber's definition.$ex48$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc48$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc48$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
