UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a48B$Naming a real collagen-derived fiber but assigning it the wrong mechanical role (soft-organ scaffolding network, not tensile load-bearing)$a48B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a48D$Selecting a real matrix component that resists the wrong load type (compression, via hydrated proteoglycans) and is not a fiber at all$a48D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Tissues: Connective Tissue · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g49)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Connective Tissue',
    $qt49$A histologist examines a developing salivary gland and finds, in the loose tissue immediately beneath the secretory epithelial cells, a spindle-shaped cell that is actively synthesizing and exporting collagen fibers and the polysaccharide-rich ground substance that together build the supportive stroma surrounding the gland's epithelial parenchyma. Which connective-tissue cell is the histologist MOST likely observing?$qt49$,
    $op49$[{"label":"A","text":"A mast cell, because it discharges granule-stored histamine and heparin that build the surrounding matrix"},{"label":"B","text":"A macrophage, because it migrates in from the blood and lays down collagen while clearing cellular debris"},{"label":"C","text":"An adipocyte, because it stores lipid droplets that diffuse outward to form the ground substance"},{"label":"D","text":"A fibroblast, because it secretes the collagen fibers and ground substance that form the connective-tissue matrix supporting the epithelium"}]$op49$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex49$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because the fibroblast is the connective-tissue-proper cell that synthesizes and secretes both the protein fibers (such as collagen) and the polysaccharide-rich ground substance that together make up the extracellular matrix forming the supportive stroma beneath an epithelium. The text states that "the most abundant cell in connective tissue proper is the fibroblast. Polysaccharides and proteins secreted by fibroblasts combine with extra-cellular fluids to produce a viscous ground substance that, with embedded fibrous proteins, forms the extra-cellular matrix," and separately that "three main types of fibers are secreted by fibroblasts: collagen fibers, elastic fibers, and reticular fibers." This matrix-secreting, supportive role is exactly the stroma that surrounds and supports the epithelial parenchyma of a gland, so the cell being observed must be the fibroblast. (Choice A) The mast cell is incorrect: its cytoplasmic granules "contain the chemical signals histamine and heparin," inflammatory mediators it releases when irritated or damaged, not collagen or ground substance, so it does not build the matrix. (Choice B) The macrophage is incorrect: it "is a large cell derived from a monocyte" that enters from the blood vessels and functions in immune defense, "engulfing infectious agents and cellular debris," rather than depositing the structural matrix. (Choice C) The adipocyte is incorrect: adipocytes "are cells that store lipids as droplets that fill most of the cytoplasm" for energy storage and insulation, and stored lipid is not secreted to form the ground substance. (Choice D) The fibroblast is correct because it is the cell whose secreted fibers and ground substance constitute the supportive connective-tissue matrix beneath the epithelium. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must take an observed cellular activity (secretion of collagen plus ground substance to build supportive stroma) and reason backward to identify which of several connective-tissue cell types performs that specific matrix-building role.$ex49$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc49$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc49$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a49A$Names a real connective-tissue-proper cell (mast cell) but attributes the matrix-building function to its granule contents (histamine/heparin), which are inflammatory mediators, not structural matrix.$a49A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a49B$Assigns the fibroblast's collagen-depositing role to the macrophage, conflating the immune/phagocytic resident cell with the matrix-secreting cell.$a49B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a49C$Confuses the adipocyte's lipid-storage product with the secreted polysaccharide ground substance, treating stored fat as if it became extracellular matrix.$a49C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Tissues: Connective Tissue · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g50)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Connective Tissue',
    $qt50$A mutation in a patient's large elastic arteries severely reduces the amount of functional elastin deposited in the vessel wall, while the collagen fibers and ground substance are laid down normally. With each ventricular contraction, the arterial wall still stretches as blood is ejected. Which consequence for the affected artery most directly follows from the elastin defect?$qt50$,
    $op50$[{"label":"A","text":"After each systolic stretch the wall fails to spring back to its original diameter, so it loses the recoil that normally helps propel blood forward between heartbeats."},{"label":"B","text":"The wall ruptures under normal systolic pressure because it can no longer resist being stretched, having lost its tensile strength."},{"label":"C","text":"The wall can no longer trap water in its matrix, so the vessel collapses from loss of the turgor that ground substance normally provides."},{"label":"D","text":"The endothelium lining the lumen sloughs away because elastin is the protein that anchors these epithelial cells to the vessel surface."}]$op50$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex50$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because elastin is the fiber that lets a stretched wall return to its original shape, so losing it specifically abolishes recoil while leaving the wall otherwise structurally laid down. The text states that "The main property of elastin is that after being stretched or compressed, it will return to its original shape," and that "Dense irregular elastic tissues give arterial walls the strength and the ability to regain original shape after stretching." In a large elastic artery, this recoil is what stores the energy of systolic stretch and releases it between beats to keep blood moving forward; remove functional elastin and the wall still stretches during systole but cannot spring back, so it loses recoil while collagen and ground substance keep their normal roles. (Choice A) Correct: with elastin gone, the wall stretches but does not return to its original diameter, which is exactly the loss of recoil the text attributes to elastin. (Choice B) Wrong: resisting stretch and providing tensile strength are properties of collagen fibers, which "have great tensile strength, resist stretching"; because collagen is laid down normally here, the wall still resists stretch, so rupture is not the direct consequence of the elastin defect — this is a collagen-versus-elastin swap. (Choice C) Wrong: trapping moisture is a property of the proteoglycan ground substance, which "attracts and traps available moisture," not of elastin; ground substance is deposited normally in this patient, so water retention is unaffected. (Choice D) Wrong: the vessel lumen is lined by endothelium, a type of epithelium, but elastin is a connective-tissue fiber that confers recoil, not the basal-lamina anchor that attaches epithelial cells — this conflates the connective-tissue defect with an unrelated epithelial readback. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the specific structure–function role of elastin to predict the physiological outcome of its loss in a particular vessel and rule out properties that belong to other matrix components.$ex50$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc50$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc50$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a50B$Attributes elastin's missing recoil to the wrong fiber — assigns tensile strength / stretch resistance (a collagen property) to elastin, predicting rupture instead of loss of recoil$a50B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a50C$Swaps in a real but unrelated matrix property — water-trapping by proteoglycan ground substance — as if it depended on elastin$a50C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a50D$Epithelial readback trap: blames endothelial detachment, conflating a connective-tissue fiber defect with epithelial basal-lamina anchorage$a50D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · easy · Skill 1 · ans D] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt41$A histologist examines a thin sheet of tightly packed cells that lines the lumen of the small intestine; the sheet sits directly on a basement membrane, contains almost no extracellular material between its cells, and has no blood vessels running through it. Which role is this tissue MOST directly specialized to perform?$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"Binding and physically connecting separate organs to one another using abundant extracellular matrix"},{"label":"B","text":"Generating force by contracting in response to electrochemical stimulation"},{"label":"C","text":"Propagating nerve impulses to communicate between distant regions of the body"},{"label":"D","text":"Covering surfaces, lining the body's cavities and passageways, and forming glands"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex41$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because the described features — a sheet of tightly packed cells with little extracellular material, resting on a basement membrane, lining a body cavity (the gut lumen) — are the defining hallmarks of epithelial tissue, whose function is to cover surfaces, line cavities and passageways, and form glands. The text states that epithelium "refers to the sheets of cells that cover exterior surfaces of the body, line internal cavities and passageways, and form certain glands" and that this tissue "is highly cellular, with little or no extracellular material present between cells," attaching to a "basal lamina ... separating it from underlying connective tissue." The avascular detail also fits: "no blood vessels cross the basement membrane to enter the tissue." (Choice A) describes connective tissue, which "binds the cells and organs of the body together" and is defined by its abundant extracellular matrix — the opposite of the cell-packed, matrix-poor sheet described. (Choice B) describes muscle tissue, which "is excitable, responding to stimulation and contracting to provide movement," not the lining sheet described. (Choice C) describes nervous tissue, which allows "the propagation of electrochemical signals in the form of nerve impulses." (Choice D) is correct because covering, lining, and gland formation atop a basement membrane is the textbook-defining role of epithelium. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the defining structural features and functions that distinguish epithelial tissue from the other three primary tissue types.$ex41$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc41$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc41$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a41A$Names connective tissue's binding/matrix role — a real but different primary tissue, tempting because both line/support the gut wall$a41A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a41B$Names muscle tissue's contractile role — real biology, another of the four primary tissues$a41B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a41C$Names nervous tissue's impulse-propagation role — real biology, another of the four primary tissues$a41C$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · easy · Skill 1 · ans A] (g42)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt42$A histologist examines the epithelium lining the trachea and notes that every cell contacts the basal lamina, yet the nuclei sit at several different heights, giving the sheet a layered appearance even though it is only one cell thick. Based on these observations, this epithelium is most accurately classified as:$qt42$,
    $op42$[{"label":"A","text":"pseudostratified, because it is a single layer of cells that only appears stratified due to nuclei lying at different levels"},{"label":"B","text":"stratified, because the multiple levels of nuclei indicate that the cells are stacked in more than one true layer"},{"label":"C","text":"simple cuboidal, because the cells form one layer and the staggered nuclei reflect a box-like cell shape"},{"label":"D","text":"transitional, because the cells change shape to produce the staggered, layered appearance of the nuclei"}]$op42$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex42$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because an epithelium in which every cell touches the basal lamina but nuclei sit at different heights — so it is one layer thick yet looks layered — is by definition pseudostratified. The number of cell layers is the classifying criterion: a single layer in which every cell rests on the basal lamina is a simple epithelium, more than one layer is stratified, and pseudostratified "describes tissue with a single layer of irregularly shaped cells that give the appearance of more than one layer" (lines 6530-6533). The text confirms that "in pseudostratified epithelium, nuclei of neighboring cells appear at different levels... all the cells are in contact with the basal lamina, although some do not reach the apical surface," and that this tissue "is found in the respiratory tract" (lines 6569-6574). The two observations in the stem — every cell on the basal lamina (one true layer) plus nuclei at staggered heights (false appearance of layering) — map exactly onto this definition. (Choice A) Correct: a single layer whose staggered nuclei merely mimic stratification is the defining feature of pseudostratified epithelium, matching the trachea lining. (Choice B) Incorrect: in a truly stratified epithelium "only the basal layer of cells rests on the basal lamina" (lines 6531-6532), but here every cell contacts the basal lamina, so it is not multilayered despite the staggered nuclei. (Choice C) Incorrect: although simple cuboidal is one layer, "the nucleus of the box-like cells appears round and is generally located near the center of the cell" (lines 6554-6555), not staggered at different heights, so it would not produce a layered appearance. (Choice D) Incorrect: transitional "describes a form of specialized stratified epithelium in which the shape of the cells can vary" (lines 6533-6534) — it is multilayered, and its hallmark is changing cell shape, not a one-cell-thick sheet with offset nuclei. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how epithelia are classified by their number of cell layers and the defining features of pseudostratified epithelium.$ex42$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc42$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc42$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a42B$Assuming nuclei at multiple levels prove true multilayering (stratified) rather than a single layer that merely looks layered$a42B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a42C$Correctly noting a single layer but assigning the wrong cell-shape category with mismatched nuclear-position reasoning$a42C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a42D$Confusing the variable-cell-shape feature of transitional (a stratified type) with the staggered-nucleus appearance of pseudostratified$a42D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · medium · Skill 2 · ans B] (g43)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt43$A histologist examines a cross-section of a kidney tubule and notes that the single layer of cells lining its lumen are box-like, each cell being about as wide as it is tall, with a round nucleus positioned near the cell's center. Based on cell shape, this epithelium is classified as:$qt43$,
    $op43$[{"label":"A","text":"squamous, because the cells are flattened and thin like scales"},{"label":"B","text":"cuboidal, because the cells are boxy and roughly as wide as they are tall"},{"label":"C","text":"columnar, because the cells are rectangular and taller than they are wide"},{"label":"D","text":"transitional, because the cells change shape as the tubule stretches and recoils"}]$op43$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex43$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because epithelia are classified by cell shape, and cells that are boxy and roughly as wide as they are tall are defined as cuboidal. The text states that "Cell shapes can be squamous (flattened and thin), cuboidal (boxy, as wide as it is tall), or columnar (rectangular, taller than it is wide)" (line 6529). It further notes that "In simple cuboidal epithelium, the nucleus of the box-like cells appears round and is generally located near the center of the cell" and that "Simple cuboidal epithelia are observed in the lining of the kidney tubules and in the ducts of glands" (lines 6554-6560). The described structure — a single layer of box-like cells, as wide as tall, with a round central nucleus, lining a kidney tubule — matches the cuboidal definition on every feature, so B is correct. (Choice A) Squamous is incorrect because squamous cells are "flattened and thin," with nuclei that "tend to be flat, horizontal, and elliptical" (lines 6529, 6544) — the opposite of the boxy, equal-dimensioned cells described. (Choice B) Cuboidal is correct because the cells are boxy, as wide as they are tall, with a round central nucleus, which is the defining description of cuboidal cells lining kidney tubules (lines 6529, 6554-6560). (Choice C) Columnar is incorrect because columnar cells are "rectangular, taller than it is wide" with an elongated, basally located nucleus (lines 6529, 6562) — the cells here are equal in width and height with a central round nucleus, not tall and elongated. (Choice D) Transitional is incorrect because that term "describes a form of specialized stratified epithelium in which the shape of the cells can vary" (lines 6533-6534); the question describes a fixed, defined shape in a single layer, not a variable, stratified arrangement. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the shape-based classification scheme to a set of described cell dimensions and select the matching category.$ex43$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc43$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc43$, 'biology', 2, 100
  ) RETURNING id
)
