SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a43A$naming the squamous (flat/thin) shape class, a real epithelial category but mismatched to the boxy cells described$a43A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a43C$naming the columnar (tall/elongated) shape class, a real epithelial category but mismatched to the equal-dimensioned cells described$a43C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a43D$naming transitional (variable-shape stratified) epithelium, treating a fixed single-layer shape as the shifting transitional type$a43D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g44)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt44$A researcher applies a fluorescent probe to a sheet of intestinal columnar absorptive cells; the probe binds only the integrin proteins of hemidesmosomes that anchor a cell to its basement membrane, and it labels exactly one face of each cell. The microvilli forming the brush border are most likely located on the:$qt44$,
    $op44$[{"label":"A","text":"same face the probe labels, because the brush border is the structure that anchors each cell to the basement membrane."},{"label":"B","text":"lateral surfaces between adjacent cells, where tight junctions seal the intercellular space and divide the cell into compartments."},{"label":"C","text":"opposite face from the one the probe labels — the apical surface facing the intestinal lumen."},{"label":"D","text":"opposite face from the one the probe labels, oriented toward the underlying connective tissue and its blood supply."}]$op44$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex44$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the probe marks the basal face (the side held to the basement membrane), so the brush border of absorptive microvilli must be on the opposite, apical face — the surface exposed to the intestinal lumen. Epithelial cells are polarized: the textbook states they "exhibit polarity with differences in structure and function between the exposed or apical facing surface of the cell and the basal surface close to the underlying body structures," and that "the basal lamina ... provides an attachment site for the epithelium, separating it from underlying connective tissue" (forming the basement membrane). Because the probe binds the hemidesmosome integrins that "link cells to the extracellular matrix, for example, the basal lamina," it labels the basal face. The absorptive microvilli are described as "cylindrical apical surface extensions of the plasma membrane of the mucosa's epithelial cells," whose combined "mass of bristles ... is termed the brush border" and which "greatly enhanc[e] absorption" — so they sit on the apical face, opposite the labeled basal side, facing the lumen from which nutrients are absorbed. (Choice A) Reversed: the brush border absorbs nutrients at the apical surface and does not anchor the cell; anchoring to the basement membrane is done by basal hemidesmosomes, the very structures the probe labels, so the brush border is on the opposite face. (Choice B) The lateral surfaces carry junctions, but the text places microvilli/cilia "on the apical surface," not laterally; tight junctions "separate the cells into apical and basal compartments" rather than housing the brush border. (Choice C) Correct: the probe-labeled basal face anchors to the basement membrane, so the apical, lumen-facing surface bears the absorptive microvilli. (Choice D) Partial truth — it correctly puts the brush border opposite the probe, but it reverses the orientation: the face away from the basement membrane is the apical surface facing the lumen, not a second surface facing the underlying connective tissue and blood supply (that is the basal side the probe already marks). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the polarity rule with the identity of the labeled marker to predict where the absorptive specialization lies.$ex44$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc44$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc44$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a44A$Treats the brush border as the cell's anchoring structure, swapping the apical absorptive role for the basal anchoring role.$a44A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'process_step_confusion', $a44B$Places the apical brush border on the lateral junction-bearing surfaces, conflating lateral cell-cell junctions with the apical specialization.$a44B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a44D$Correctly infers 'opposite the basal probe' but reverses orientation, having the apical face point toward connective tissue/blood instead of the lumen.$a44D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g45)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt45$The alveolar surface of the lung must allow oxygen and carbon dioxide to diffuse between inhaled air and the blood across the thinnest possible barrier. If a tissue engineer is selecting an epithelial type to line a synthetic alveolus so as to minimize the gas-diffusion distance, which epithelium should be chosen?$qt45$,
    $op45$[{"label":"A","text":"Simple columnar epithelium, a single layer of tall cells"},{"label":"B","text":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium, a single layer of irregularly shaped cells appearing layered"},{"label":"C","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium, several stacked layers with flattened apical cells"},{"label":"D","text":"Simple squamous epithelium, a single layer of flattened scale-like cells"}]$op45$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex45$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because a single layer of extremely flat cells gives the shortest physical path for gas molecules to cross, which is exactly what a gas-exchange surface demands. Epithelia are classified by cell shape (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and by number of layers (simple = one layer; stratified = many) [lines 6527-6534: \"Epithelial tissues are classified according to the shape of the cells and number of the cell layers... Cell shapes can be squamous (flattened and thin), cuboidal..., or columnar...; the number of cell layers... can be one... which is a simple epithelium, or more than one, which is a stratified epithelium\"]. To minimize diffusion distance you want both the fewest layers (simple) and the flattest cells (squamous). The text states this directly: \"Simple squamous epithelium, because of the thinness of the cell, is present where rapid passage of chemical compounds is observed. The alveoli of lungs where gases diffuse... are also made of simple squamous epithelial tissue\" [lines 6546-6549]. (Choice A) Simple columnar epithelium is a single layer but its cells are \"tall column-like\" and \"active in the absorption and secretion of molecules\" lining the digestive tract [lines 6562-6564]; the cell height lengthens the diffusion path, so it is wrong on the shape axis. (Choice B) Pseudostratified columnar epithelium \"is found in the respiratory tract\" [line 6573], which tempts a test-taker who keys on \"respiratory,\" but it lines the conducting airways (not the alveoli) and consists of tall columnar cells, again a longer diffusion distance. (Choice C) Stratified squamous epithelium is a genuine partial truth: like simple squamous it has flat apical cells, but it \"consists of several stacked layers of cells\" and exists to \"protect against physical and chemical wear and tear\" [lines 6599-6601]; the extra layers maximize barrier thickness, the opposite of what diffusion requires. (Choice D) Simple squamous epithelium is correct because it combines the single layer (simple) with the flattest cell shape (squamous), and the text names the alveoli as its location specifically because gases must diffuse rapidly across this thinnest barrier [lines 6546-6549]. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the functional requirement (shortest diffusion distance) with the two classification axes of epithelium (layer number and cell shape) to predict the form that best fits the function.$ex45$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc45$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc45$, 'biology', 2, 135
  ) RETURNING id
)
