UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a45B$Lured by 'respiratory tract' association, but names the conducting-airway lining (tall, appears layered) rather than the thin alveolar surface$a45B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a45C$Correct flat (squamous) apical shape and truly 'protective,' but multilayered (stratified) = maximal barrier thickness, the opposite of what diffusion needs$a45C$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [Tissues: Epithelial Tissue · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g46)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Tissues: Epithelial Tissue',
    $qt46$The esophagus conveys swallowed boluses of coarse food to the stomach, and its luminal surface is repeatedly scraped by this passing material. An epithelium that lines this surface must therefore withstand ongoing mechanical abrasion without exposing the underlying tissue. Which epithelial form is best suited to line the esophageal lumen?$qt46$,
    $op46$[{"label":"A","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium, because its multiple stacked cell layers can be abraded away from the surface while deeper layers continue to shield the underlying tissue"},{"label":"B","text":"Simple squamous epithelium, because its flat, scale-like cells form a smooth surface over which food can slide with minimal friction"},{"label":"C","text":"Simple columnar epithelium, because its tall absorptive cells maximize uptake of nutrients from food as it passes"},{"label":"D","text":"Transitional epithelium, because its cells change shape to accommodate the changing volume of material moving through the lumen"}]$op46$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex46$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because a surface exposed to ongoing mechanical abrasion requires several stacked cell layers, so that surface cells can be sloughed off and replaced from below without breaching the protective barrier. The text states that stratified epithelium "consists of several stacked layers of cells" and that "this epithelium protects against physical and chemical wear and tear," and it names the mouth lining—an abrasion-exposed digestive surface like the esophagus—as "an example of an unkeratinized, stratified squamous epithelium." Naming is by the shape of the most apical layer, so the flattened apical cells make it stratified squamous. A single-layered epithelium, by contrast, would be breached the moment its one cell layer was scraped, exposing the underlying connective tissue. (Choice A) Correct: stratified squamous epithelium combines the squamous apical shape with multiple layers, so surface cells can be lost to abrasion while the deeper layers maintain the barrier. (Choice B) Tempting but wrong: the squamous (flat, scale-like) shape is correct, but a simple epithelium is "a single layer of cells," and the text notes simple squamous epithelium occurs "where rapid passage of chemical compounds is observed" because of "the thinness of the cell"—a one-cell-thick sheet would be destroyed by abrasion rather than protect against it. (Choice C) Simple columnar epithelium "is active in the absorption and secretion of molecules" and lines absorptive regions of the digestive tract, but a single layer optimized for uptake cannot withstand mechanical wear. (Choice D) Transitional epithelium is "found only in the urinary system" and is specialized to stretch as the bladder fills, not to resist abrasion from passing solids. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must reason from a functional demand (resisting mechanical abrasion) to the epithelial form that satisfies it, rather than recall a stated fact.$ex46$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc46$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc46$, 'biology', 2, 135
  ) RETURNING id
)
